                "annotations": [],
                "text": "Analyse the training examples:\n\nIn each example:\n- There is a multi-color shape / pattern (which doesn't contain color 5 / grey).\n- There is one or more grey (5) pixels.\n- The output replaces the grey pixel with the shape, or copies the shape to the location of the grey pixel, while keeping the original shape intact.\n- Let's check the alignment/anchor:\n  - Example 1:\n    - Shape:\n      Row 1: [2, 3] at cols 1, 2\n      Row 2: [4, 1] at cols 2, 3\n      Row 3: [7] at col 2\n      Bounding box: r: 1..3, c: 1..3\n      Colors:\n      (1,1): 2, (1,2): 3\n      (2,2): 4, (2,3): 1\n      (3,2): 7\n    - Grey pixel: at (5, 6).\n    - In output:\n      (4,5): 2, (4,6): 3\n      (5,6): 4, (5,7): 1\n      (6,6): 7\n      Notice where (5,6) is: it corresponds to (2,2) of the original shape!\n      What is at (2,2)? It's color 4.\n      Wait, why does (5,6) align with (2,2)?\n      Is (2,2) the center?\n      Shape bounding box is 3x3: r in [1,3], c in [1,3]. The center is (2,2)!\n      Or is it the center of mass, or center of bounding box?\n  - Example 2:\n    - Grey pixels:\n      (1, 1) and (4, 7).\n    - Shape:\n      Row 6: [8] at c=2\n      Row 7: [6, 2, 9] at c=1, 2, 3\n      Row 8: [6] at c=1\n      Bounding box: r: 6..8 (rows 6, 7, 8 -> height 3), c: 1..3 (cols 1, 2, 3 -> width 3).\n      Center of bounding box: (7, 2). At (7,2) we have 2.\n    - Let's check copies in output:\n      Copy 1:\n        Row 0: [8] at c=1\n        Row 1: [6, 2, 9] at c=0, 1, 2\n        Row 2: [6] at c=0\n        The center is at (1, 1). Grey pixel was at (1, 1)!\n      Copy 2:\n        Row 3: [8] at c=7\n        Row 4: [6, 2, 9] at c=6, 7, 8\n        Row 5: [6] at c=6\n        The center is at (4, 7). Grey pixel was at (4, 7)!\n  - Example 3:\n    - Shape:\n      Rows 1..3, Cols 5..7 (3x3 bounding box).\n      Center is (2, 6).\n    - Grey pixel at (6, 2).\n    - Output copy:\n      Rows 5..7, Cols 1..3 (3x3).\n      Center is (6, 2).\n      Shape is copied such that the center of the bounding box is placed at the grey pixel!\n\nSo the rule is:\n1. Identify the shape (non-zero, non-5 pixels).\n2. Compute the bounding box of the shape: `r_min, r_max, c_min, c_max`.\n3. Center of the bounding box is `r_center = (r_min + r_max) // 2`, `c_center = (c_min + c_max) // 2`.\n4. For each grey pixel (5) at `(gr, gc)`:\n   - Paste the shape such that `r_center, c_center` maps to `gr, gc`.\n   - Specifically, for each pixel `(r, c)` of the shape, put it at `(r - r_center + gr, c - c_center + gc)`.\n5. Remove the 5s (they get overwritten or cleared if not covered? Wait, in all examples they are covered by the shape).\n6. Keep the original shape.\n\nReady for the test input!",
